language ESSENCE' 1.0
$ assignment_costs: an Essence' model from DCP Rosetta.
$
$ Assign people to 4 tasks with different costs, so that the total cost is minimised. Each task must be assigned to exactly one person, and each person takes at most one task; it is not necessary to assign all people to a task.
$
$ Save this file as model.eprime. Download `assignment_costs.json` and `dcp_convert_essence.py` from the
$ problem's page:
$   https://dcp-bench.github.io/DCP-Rosetta/problems/assignment_costs.html
$ Convert the instances once, then run the first one with Savile Row 1.11 (it needs Java); the
$ solution is written to `assignment_costs_1.param.solution`:
$   python dcp_convert_essence.py assignment_costs.json
$   savilerow model.eprime assignment_costs_1.param -run-solver

$ number of tasks (rows of cost), derived from the data
given cost_dim1 : int
$ number of people (columns of cost), derived from the data
given cost_dim2 : int
$ cost[i,j]: cost of task i done by person j, indices 1-based
given cost : matrix indexed by [int(1..cost_dim1), int(1..cost_dim2)] of int

$ x[i,j]: task i is assigned to person j
find x : matrix indexed by [int(1..cost_dim1), int(1..cost_dim2)] of bool

branching on [x]

minimising (sum i : int(1..cost_dim1) . sum j : int(1..cost_dim2) . toInt(x[i,j]) * cost[i,j])
such that
$ each task is assigned to exactly one person
forAll i : int(1..cost_dim1) . (sum j : int(1..cost_dim2) . toInt(x[i,j])) = 1,
$ each person takes at most one task
forAll j : int(1..cost_dim2) . (sum i : int(1..cost_dim1) . toInt(x[i,j])) <= 1
